\begin_layout Title
Chapter 9 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Economic Core, Fair Allocations, and Social Choice Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
9.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this chapter, we briefly discuss some topics in the framework of general
 equilibrium theory, namely economic core, fair allocations, and social
 choice theory.
 The theory of core is important because it gives an insight into how a
 competitive equilibrium is achieved as a result of individual strategic
 behavior instead of results of an auctioneer and the Walrasian tatonnement
 mechanism.
 It shows the necessity of adopting a market institution as long as individuals
 behave self-interestedly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have also seen that Pareto optimality may be too weka a criterion to
 be meaningful.
 It does not address any question about income distribution and equity of
 allocations.
 Fairness is a notion to overcome this difficulty.
 This is one way to restrict a set of Pareto optimum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In a slightly different framework, suppose that a society is deciding the
 social priority among finite alternatives.
 Alternatives may be different from Pareto optimal allocations.
 Let us think of a social 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

rule
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to construct the social ordering (social welfare function) from many individual
 orderings of different alternatives.
 The question is: is it possible to construct a rule satisfying several
 desirable properties? Both 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fairness
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

social welfare function
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 address a question of social justice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
9.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Core of Exchange Economies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The use of a competitive (market) system is just one way to allocate resources.
 What if we use some other social institution? Would we still end up with
 an allocaiton that was 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

close
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to a competitive equilibrium allocation? The answer will be that, if we
 allow agents to form coalitions, the resulting allocaiton can only be a
 competitive equilibrium allocaiton when the economy becomes large.
 Such an allocation is called a core allocation and was originally considered
 by Edgeworth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The core is a concept in which every individual and every group agree to
 accept an allocation instead of moving away from the social coalition.
 There is some reason to think that the core is a meaningful political concept.
 If a group of people find themselves able, using their own resources to
 achieve a better life, it is not unreasonable to suppose that they will
 try to enforce this threat against the rest of the community.
 They may find themselves frustrated if the rest of the community resorts
 to violence or force to prevent them from withdrawing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The theory of the core is distinguished by its parsimony.
 Its conceptual apparatus does not appeal to any specific trading mechanism,
 nor does it assume any particular institutional setup.
 Informally, the notion of competition that the theory explores is one in
 which traders are well informed of the economic characteristics of other
 traders, and in which the members of any group of traders can bind themselves
 to any mutually advantageous agreement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For simplicity, we consider exchange economies.
 We say two agents are of the same type if they have the same preferences
 and endowments.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

-replication of the original economy: there are 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 times as many agents of each type in the original economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.2.1 (Blocking Coalition).

\series default
 A group of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 (a coalition) is said to block (improve upon) a given allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 if there is a Pareto imporvement with their own resources, that is, if
 there is some allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
it is feasible for 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in S}x_{i}^{\prime}\leq\sum_{i\in S}w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}^{\prime}\succ_{k}x_{k}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A 
\emph on
coalition
\emph default
 is a group of agents, and thus it is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 agents.
 If the original economy has 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 agents, then 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{N}-1$
\end_inset

 possible coalitions can be formed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.2.3 (Core).

\series default
 A feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to have the core property if it cannot be improved upon for any
 coalition.
 The core of an economy is the set of all feasible allocations that have
 the core property, i.e., it is the set of allocations that have no blocking
 coalitions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.2.1.
 
\series default
Every allocation in the core is Pareto optimal (coalition by whole people),
 but not every Pareto optimal allocation is in the core.
 So, the set of core allocations is a subset of the set of Pareto efficient
 allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.2.3 (Individual Rationality).

\series default
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is individually rational if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim_{i}w_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The individual rationality condition is also called the participation condition
 which means that a person will not participate in the economic activity
 if he is worse off than at the initial endowment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.2.2.

\series default
 Every allocation in the core must be individually rational.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.2.3.

\series default
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

 and preference relations are weakly monotonic, an allocation is in the
 core if and only if it is Pareto optimal and individually rational.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.2.4.

\series default
 Even though a Pareto optimal allocation is independent of individual endowments
, an allocation in the core depends on individual endowments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What is the relationship between core allocaitons and competitive equilibrium
 allocations?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
9.2.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Under local non-satiation, if 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,p)$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 has the core property.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfy local non-satiation.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,p)$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 does not have the core property.
 Then, there exists a group of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 and an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in S}x_{i}^{\prime}\leq\sum_{i\in S}w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}^{\prime}\succ_{k}x_{k}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in S$
\end_inset

.
 By local non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in S:p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}\geq p\cdot x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 By utility maximization, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k\in S:p\cdot x_{k}^{\prime}>p\cdot x_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Taking summation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i\in S}p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}>\sum_{i\in S}p\cdot x_{i}=\sum_{i\in S}p\cdot w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is feasible for 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the competitive equilibrium must be an allocation in the core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We refer to the allocations in which consumers of the same type get the
 same consumption bundles as 
\emph on
equal-treatment allocations
\emph default
.
 It can be shown that any allocation in the core must be an equal-treatment
 allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
9.2.1 (Equal Treatment in the Core).
 
\series default
\emph on
Suppose agents' preferences are strictly convex.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is an allocation in the r-core of a given economy, then any two agents
 of the same type must receive the same bundle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume preferences are strictly convex.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be an allocation in the core and index the 
\begin_inset Formula $2r$
\end_inset

 agents using subscripts 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{1},\ldots,A_{r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{1},\ldots,B_{r}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that all agents of the same type do not get the
 same allocation.
 Then, there will be at least one person of each type who does strictly
 worse than everyone else.
 Denote the allocations of these two individuals as 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{uA}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{uB}$
\end_inset

.
 If there are ties, select any of the tied agents.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{A}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{i=1}^{r}x_{A_{i}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{B}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{i=1}^{r}x_{B_{i}}$
\end_inset

 be the average bundle of the type-
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and type-
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 agents.
 Since the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is feasible and every agent of the same type gets the same endowment, we
 have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{x}_{A}+\bar{x}_{B}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{i=1}^{r}x_{A_{i}}+\frac{1}{r}\sum_{i=1}^{r}x_{B_{i}}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{i=1}^{r}w_{A_{i}}+\frac{1}{r}\sum_{i=1}^{r}w_{B_{i}}=\frac{1}{r}rw_{A}+\frac{1}{r}rw_{B}=w_{A}+w_{B}
\]

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\bar{x}_{A},\bar{x}_{B})$
\end_inset

 is feasible for the coalition consisting of the two underdogs.
 Remember, we are assuming that at least one of the underdogs receives a
 different bundle than the rest of the agents of the same type.
 Without loss of generality, let the 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 underdog receive a different bundle than the rest of the 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 agents.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{A}$
\end_inset

 is the weighted average of bundles that are at least as good as 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{uA}$
\end_inset

, then underdog 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 strictly prefers 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{A}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{uA}$
\end_inset

 by strict convexity of preferences.
 Moreover, the 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 underdog finds 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{B}$
\end_inset

 at least as good as 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{uB}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, underdog 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and underdog 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 can form a coalition that can impove upon the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 has the core property.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since any allocaiton in the core must award agents of the same type with
 the same bundle, we can examine the cores of relicated two-agent economies
 by use of the Edgeworth box diagram.
 Instead of a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in the core representing how much 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 gets and how much 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 gets, we can think of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 as telling us how much each agent of type 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 getd and how much each agent of type 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 gets.
 The above proposition tells us that all points in the 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

-core can be represented in this manner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following theorem is a converse of Theorem 9.2.1 and shows that any allocation
 that is not a market equilibrium allocation must eventually not be in the
 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

-core of the economy.
 This means that core allocations in large economies look just like Walrasian
 equilibria.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
9.2.2 (Shrinking Core Theorem).

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are strictly convex and continuous.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

is a unique competitive equilibrium allocation.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is not a competitive equilibrium, then there is some replication 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is not in the 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

-core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
As we increase the 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 replication of the economy, any point originally in the core that is not
 a CE will no longer be in the core as the economy grows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 be strictly convex and continuous.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a unique competitive equilibrium.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is not a competitive equilibrium allocation.
 We want to show that there is a coalition such that the point 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 can be improved upon for 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

-replication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is not a competitive equilibrium, the line segment through 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 must cut at least one agent's indifferenece curve through 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 If the line segment was tangent to both indifference curves at 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 would be a competitive equilibrium.
 Without loss of generality, let the line segment cut through agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

's indifference curve through 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 So, by strict convexity and continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

, there are integers 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $0<T<V$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
g_{A}\equiv\frac{T}{V}w_{A}+(1-\frac{T}{V})y_{A}\succ_{A}y_{A}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, form a coalition consisting of 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 consumers of type 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V-T$
\end_inset

 consumers of type 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 and consider the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x=(g_{A},\ldots,g_{A},y_{B},\ldots,y_{B})$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is feasible for this coalition.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{B}$
\end_inset

 are the allocations before the coalition.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Vg_{A}+(V-T)y_{B} & = & V\left[\frac{T}{V}w_{A}+(1-\frac{T}{V})y_{A}\right]+(V-T)y_{B}\\
 & = & Tw_{A}+Vy_{A}-Ty_{A}+Vy_{B}-Ty_{B}\\
 & = & Tw_{A}+(V-T)y_{A}+(V-T)y_{B}\\
 & = & Tw_{A}+(V-T)(y_{A}+y_{B})\\
 & = & Tw_{A}+(V-T)(w_{A}+w_{B})\\
 & = & Vw_{A}+(V-T)w_{B}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Important to note, since 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is in the core before replication, then it is Pareto optimal, and hence,
 feasible.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{A}+w_{B}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is feasible in the coalition and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{A}\succ_{A}y_{A}$
\end_inset

 for all agents in type 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{B}\sim_{B}y_{B}$
\end_inset

 for all agents in type 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is not in the 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

-core for the 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

-replication of the economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.2.5.

\series default
 Thus, as we increase the 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 replication of the economy, any point originally in the core that's not
 a competitive equilibrium will, eventually, no longer be in the core as
 the economy grows.
 The shrinking core theorem then shows that the only allocations that are
 in the core of a large economy are market equilibrium allocations, and
 thus Walrasian equilibria are robust: even very weak equilibrium concepts,
 like that of the core, tend to yield allocations that are close to Walrasian
 equilibria for larger economies.
 Thus, this theorem shows the essential importance of competition and full
 economic freedom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.2.6.

\series default
 Many of the restrictive assumptions in this proposition can be relaxed
 such as strict monotonicity, convexity, uniqueness of competitive equilibrium,
 and two types of agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
9.2.3 (Limit Theorem on the Core).

\series default
 
\emph on
Under strict convexity and continuitiy, the core of a replicated two person
 economy shrinks when the number of agents for each type increases, and
 the core coincides with the competitive equilibrium allocation if the number
 of agents goes to infinity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This result means that any allocation which is not a competitive equilibrium
 allocation is not in the core for some 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

- replication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
9.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Fairness of Allocations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.3.1 (Envy).

\series default
 An agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is said to envy agent 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 if agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 prefers agent 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

's consumption, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Pareto efficiency gives a criterion of how the goods are allocated efficiently,
 but it may be too weak a criterion to be meaningful.
 It does not address any questions about income distribution, and does not
 give any 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

equity
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 implication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.3.1.
 (Envy).

\series default
 An agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is said to envy agent 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 if agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 prefers agent 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

's consumption, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is the equitable allocation? How can we define the notion of an equitable
 allocation?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.3.2 (Envy-free, also known as Equitable).

\series default
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is envy-free, or equitable, if no one envies anyone else, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N,\forall\ k\in N:x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fairness is a notion that may overcome the difficulty just described.
 This is one way to restrict the whole set of Pareto efficient outcomes
 to a small set of Pareto efficient outcomes that satisfy the other properties.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.3.3 (Fairness).

\series default
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to be fair if it is both Pareto optimal and equitable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.3.1.

\series default
 By definition, the set of fair allocations is a subset of the set of Pareto
 efficient allocations.
 Therefore, fairness restricts the size of Pareto optimal allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.3.4 (Strictly Envy-Free or Strictly Equitably).

\series default
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is strictly equitable, or strictly envy-free, if no one envies any other
 coalition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 envies a coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(i\notin S)$
\end_inset

 at an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{S}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{S}=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{j\in S}x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.3.4 (Strict Fairness).

\series default
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to be strictly fair if it is both Pareto optimal and strictly equitable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.3.2.

\series default
 If an allocation is strictly envy-free, then it is envy-free.
 The set of strictly fair allocations are a subset of the set of Pareto
 optimal allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.3.3.

\series default
 For a two person exchange economy, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal, then it is impossible for both persons to envy each
 other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.3.4.

\series default
 It is clear that every strictly fair allocation is a fair allocation, but
 the converse may not be true.
 However, when 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

, a fair allocation is a strictly fair allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How to test a fair allocation? Let us restrict an economy to a two-person
 economy.
 An easy way for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to compare his own allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 with agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

's allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}$
\end_inset

 in the Edgeworth Box is to find a point symmetric of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 against the center of the box.
 That is, draw a line from 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 to the center of the box and extrapolate it to the other side by the same
 length to find 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, and then make the comparison.
 If the indifference curve through 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 cuts 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

below
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 envies 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have the following way to test whether an allocation is a fair allocatio
n:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is it Pareto optimal? If the answer is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then go to step 2.
 If not, then stop.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Construct a reflection point 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{B},x_{A})$
\end_inset

.
 Not that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{x_{A}+x_{B}}{2}$
\end_inset

 is the center of the Edgeworth Box.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compare 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 for person 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to see if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}\succ_{A}x_{A}$
\end_inset

 and compare 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}$
\end_inset

 for person 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to see if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}\succ_{B}x_{B}$
\end_inset

.
 If the answer is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for both persons, then it is a fair allocation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have given some desirable properties of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fair
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 allocations.
 A question is whether it exists at all.
 The following theorem provides one sufficient condition to guarantee the
 existence of fairness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
9.3.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},p^{*})$
\end_inset

 be a competitive equilibrium.
 Under local non-satiation, if all individuals' income is the same, i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot w_{1}=\cdots=p^{*}\cdot w_{n}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a strictly fair allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
By local non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is Pareto efficient.
 Now, we want to show 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is strictly equitable.
 Suppose for contradiction that it is not.
 Then, there exists an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and a coalition 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $i\notin S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{j\in S}x_{j}^{*}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}
\]

\end_inset

By utility maximizaiton and local non-satiation, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{j\in S}p\cdot x_{j}^{*}>p^{*}\cdot x_{i}^{*}=p^{*}\cdot w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

However, this contradicts the fact that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{j\in S}p^{*}\cdot x_{j}^{*}=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{j\in S}p^{*}\cdot w_{j}=p^{*}\cdot w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot w_{1}=\cdots=p^{*}\cdot w_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 must be a strictly fair allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Agents have an initial endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and then endowments are redistributed using transfer payments in such a
 way that income is the same for all individuals.
 After the redistribution, trading begins and we obtain a competitive equilibriu
m allocation, which will be strictly fair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.3.6.

\series default
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{nL}$
\end_inset

 is an equal income Walrasian allocation if there exists a price vector
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{i}\leq p\cdot\bar{w}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}$
\end_inset

 (average endowment)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}>p\cdot\bar{w}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\sum x_{i}\leq\sum w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Notice that every equal income Walrasian allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium allocation with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Agents have an initial endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

, and then it is redistributed using transfer payments such that 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 After this, trading begins and we obtain a CE allocation, which will be
 strictly fair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
9.3.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Under local non-satiation, every equal income Walrasian allocation is a
 strictly fair allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.3.5.

\series default
 An allocation that is an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

equal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 division of resources does not give 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fairness,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 but trading from 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

equal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 position will result in a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fair
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 allocation.
 This 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

divide-and-choose
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 recommendation implies that if the center of the box is chosen as the initial
 endowment point, the competitive equilibrium allocation is fair.
 A political implication of this remark is straightforward.
 Consumption of equal bundles is not Pareto optimal if preferences are different.
 However, an equal division of endowment plus competitive markets result
 in fair allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.3.6.

\series default
 A competitive equilibrium from an equitable (but not 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

equal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 division) endowment is not necessarily fair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.3.7.

\series default
 Fair allocations are defined without reference to initial endowments.
 Since we are dealing with optimality concepts, initial endowments can be
 redistributed among agents in a society.
 Agents have an initial endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

, and then it is redistributed using transfer payments such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:w_{i}=\bar{w}$
\end_inset

.
 After the redistribution, trading begins and we obtain a competitive equilibriu
m allocation, which will be strictly fair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In general, there is no relationship between an allocation in the core and
 a fair allocation.
 However, when the social endowments are divided equally among two persons,
 we have the following theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
9.3.2.

\series default
 
\emph on
In a two-person exchange economy, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are convex, and if the total endowments are equally divided among individuals,
 then the set of allocations in the core is a subset of (strictly) fair
 allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We know that an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in the core is Pareto optimal.
 We only need to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is equitable.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a core allocation, then it is individually rational.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is not equitable.
 Then, there exists some agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, say agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{B}\succ_{A}x_{A}\succsim_{A}w_{A} & = & \frac{1}{2}(w_{A}+w_{B})\\
 & = & \frac{1}{2}(x_{A}+x_{B})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

by noting that 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{A}=w_{B}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}\succsim_{A}\frac{1}{2}(x_{A}+x_{B})$
\end_inset

.
 But, on the other hand, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{A}$
\end_inset

 is convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{B}\succ_{A}x_{A}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}(x_{A}+x_{B})\succ_{A}x_{A}$
\end_inset

, which is a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
9.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Social Choice Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
9.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we present a very brief summary and introduction of social
 choice theory.
 We analyze the extent to which individual preferences can be aggregated
 into social preferences, or more directly into social decisions, in a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

satisfactory
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 manner (in a manner compatible with the fulfilment of a variety of desirable
 conditions).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As was shown in the discussion of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fairness,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 it is difficult to come up with a criterion (or a constitution) that determines
 that society's choice.
 Social choice theory aims at constructing such a rule, which could be allied
 with not only Pareto efficient allocations, but also any alternative that
 a society faces.
 We will give some fundamental results of social choice theory: Arrow's
 Impossibility Theorem, which states there does not exsts any non-dictatorial
 social welfare function satisfying a number of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reasonable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
9.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Basic Settings
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To simplify notation, 
\begin_inset Formula $aP_{i}b$
\end_inset

 means that 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 strictly prefers 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

.
 We assume that the individual preferences are all strict, in other words,
 that there is never a situation of indifference.
 For all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, one gets preference 
\begin_inset Formula $aP_{i}b$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $bP_{i}a$
\end_inset

.
 This hypothesis is not very restrictive if 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is finite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $N=\{1,\ldots,n\}$
\end_inset

: the set of individuals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $X=\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(m\geq3)$
\end_inset

: the set of alternatives (outcomes)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}=\ \succ_{i}$
\end_inset

: the strict preference orderings of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}_{i}$
\end_inset

: The class of allowed individual orderings; 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}\in\mathcal{P}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $P=(P_{1},\ldots,P_{n})$
\end_inset

: a preference ordering profile
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}$
\end_inset

: the set of all profiles of individual orderings; 
\begin_inset Formula $P\in\mathcal{P}=\mathcal{P}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{P}_{n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $S(X)$
\end_inset

: the class of allowed social orderings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Arrow's social welfare function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F:\mathcal{P}\rightarrow S(X)
\]

\end_inset

which is a mapping from individual ordering profiles to social orderings
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Gibbard-Satterthwaite's social choice function (SCF) is a mapping from individua
l preference orderings to the alternatives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f:\mathcal{P}\rightarrow X
\]

\end_inset

Note that even though individuals' preference orderings are transitive,
 a social preference ordering may not be transitive.
 To see this, consider the following example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
9.4.1.
 (The Condorcet Paradox).

\series default
 Suppose a social choice is determined by the majority voting rule.
 Does this determine a well-defined social welfare function? The answer
 in in general no by the well-known Condorcet paradox.
 Consider a society with three agents and three alternatives: 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y,z$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose each person's preferences is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x\succ_{1}y\succ_{1}z\ \mbox{(by person 1)}\\
y\succ_{2}z\succ_{2}x\ \mbox{(by person 2)}\\
z\succ_{3}x\succ_{3}y\ \mbox{(by person 3)}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

By the majority rule,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
For 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $xFy$
\end_inset

 (by social preference)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
For 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $yFz$
\end_inset

 (by social preference)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
For 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $zFx$
\end_inset

 (by social preference)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Then pairwise majority voting tells us that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 must be socially preferred to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 must be socially preferred to 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 must be socially preferred to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 This cyclic pattern means that social preference is not transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The number of preference profiles can increase very rapidly with increase
 in the number of alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
9.4.2.

\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\{x,y,z\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $n=3$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x\succ y\succ z\\
x\succ z\succ y\\
y\succ x\succ z\\
y\succ z\succ x\\
z\succ x\succ y\\
z\succ y\succ x
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, there are six possible individual orderings, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mathcal{P}_{i}\right|=6$
\end_inset

, and therefore there are 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mathcal{P}_{1}\right|\times\left|\mathcal{P}_{2}\right|\times\left|\mathcal{P}_{3}\right|=216$
\end_inset

 possible combinations of 3-individual preference orderings on three alternative
s.
 The social welfare function is a mapping from each of these 216 entries
 (cases) to one particular social ordering (among six possible social orderings
 of three alternatives).
 The social choice function is a mapping from each of these 216 cases to
 one particular choice (among three alternatives).
 A question we will investigate is what kinds of deriable conditions should
 be imposed on these social welfare or choice functions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

You may think of a hypothetical case of sending a letting to your congressional
 representative that lists your preference orderings, 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}$
\end_inset

, on announced national projections (alternatives), such as reducing deficits,
 expanding the medical program, reducing the social security program, increasing
 the national defense budget, etc.
 Congress convenes with a huge stake of letters 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 and tries to come up with national priorities 
\begin_inset Formula $F(P)$
\end_inset

.
 You want Congress to make a certain rule (i.e., the Constitution) to form
 national priorities out of individual preference orderings.
 This is a question addressed in social choice theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
9.4.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Arrow's Impossibility Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Unrestriced Domain (UD):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.4.1.
 
\series default
A class of allowed individual orderings 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}_{i}$
\end_inset

 consists of all possible orderings defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Pareto Principle (P):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.4.2.
 
\series default
If for 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y\in X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $xP_{i}y$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $xF(P)y$
\end_inset

 (social preferences).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives (IIA):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.4.3.

\series default
 For any two alternatives 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y\in X$
\end_inset

 and any two preference profiles 
\begin_inset Formula $P,P^{\prime}\in\mathcal{P}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $xF(p)y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{i:xP_{i}y\}=\{i:xP_{i}^{\prime}y\}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $xF(P^{\prime})y$
\end_inset

.
 That is, the social preference between any two alternatives depends only
 on the profile of individual preferences over the same alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.4.1.

\series default
 IIA means that the ranking between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 for any agent is equivalent in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 implies that the social ranking between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $F(P)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(P^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is the same.
 In other words, if two different preference profiles are the same on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, then the social order must be the same on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 9.4.2.

\series default
 By IIA, any change in preference ordering other than the ordering of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 should not affect social ordering between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.4.4 (Dictator).

\series default
 There is some agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $F(P)=P_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $P\in\mathcal{P}^{n}$
\end_inset

, and agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is called the dictator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
9.4.1 (Arrow's Impossibility Theorem).

\series default
 
\emph on
Any social welfare function that satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $m\geq3$
\end_inset

, UD, P, and IIA conditions is dictatorial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The impact of Arrow's Impossibility Theorem has been quite substantial.
 Obviously, the result of the theorem is a disappointment.
 The most pessimistic reaction to it is to conclude that there is just no
 acceptable way to aggregate individual preferences, and hence, no theoretical
 basis for treating welfare issues.
 A more moderate reaction, however, is to examine each of the assumptions
 of the theorem to see which might be given up.
 Conditions imposed on social welfare functions may be too restrictive.
 Indeed, when some conditions are relaxed, then the results could be positive,
 e.g., UD is usually relaxed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
9.4.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Some Positive Result: Restricted Domain
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When some of the assumptions imposed in Arrow's Impossibility Theorem are
 removed, the result may be positive.
 For instance, if alternatives have certain characteristics which could
 be placed in a spectrum, preferences may show some patterns and may not
 exhaust all possible orderings on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, thus violated UD.
 In the following setup, we consider the case of a restricted domain.
 A famous example is a class of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

single-peaked
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 preferences.
 We show that under the assumption of single-peaked preferences, non-dictatorial
 aggregation is possible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.4.5.

\series default
 A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
linear order
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 is reflexive 
\begin_inset Formula $(x\geq x)$
\end_inset

, transitive 
\begin_inset Formula $(x\geq y\geq z\implies x\geq z)$
\end_inset

, and total 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mbox{for distinct}\ x,y\in X:x\geq y\ \vee\ y\geq x,\ \mbox{but not both})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\geq y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.4.6.

\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
single-peaked
\emph default
 with respect to the linear order 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, if there is an alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is increasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the lower contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $L(x)=\{y\in X:y\leq x\}$
\end_inset

 and decreasing with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 on the upper contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $U(x)=\{y\in X:y\geq x\}$
\end_inset

.
 That is
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x\geq z>y\implies z\succ_{i}y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $y>z\geq x\implies z\succ_{i}y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
In words, there is an alternative that represents a peak of satisfaction.
 Moreover, satisfaction increases as we approach this peak so that, in particula
r, there cannot be any other peak of satisfaction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given a profile of preference 
\begin_inset Formula $(\succsim_{1},\ldots,\succsim_{n})$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the maximal alternative for 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 (we will say that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's peak
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
9.4.7.

\series default
 Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $h\in N$
\end_inset

 is a median agent for the profile 
\begin_inset Formula $(\succsim_{1},\ldots,\succsim_{n})$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\#\{i\in N:x_{i}\geq x_{h}\}\geq\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\#\{i\in N:x_{h}\geq x_{i}\}\geq\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
9.4.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 is a linear order on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is single-peaked.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $h\in N$
\end_inset

 be a median agent, then the majority rule 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{F}(\succsim)$
\end_inset

 is aggregatable:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ y\in X:x_{h}\tilde{F}(\succsim)y
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Take any 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in X$
\end_inset

 and suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{h}>y$
\end_inset

 (the argument is symmetric for 
\begin_inset Formula $y>x_{h}$
\end_inset

).
 We need to show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\#\{i\in N:x_{h}\succ_{i}y\}\geq\#\{i\in N:y\succ_{i}x_{h}\}
\]

\end_inset

Consider the set of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $S\subset N$
\end_inset

 that have peaks larger or equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{h}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $S=\#\{i\in N:x_{i}\geq x_{h}\}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in S:x_{i}\geq x_{h}>y$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, by single-peakness of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in S:x_{h}\succ_{i}y$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\#S\leq\#\{i\in N:x_{h}\succ_{i}y\}$
\end_inset

.
 On the other hand, because 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is the median agent, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $\#S\geq\frac{n}{2}$
\end_inset

 and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\#\{i\in N:y\succ_{i}x_{h}\}\leq\#(N\backslash S)\leq\frac{n}{2}\leq\#S\leq\#\{i\in N:x_{h}\succ_{i}y\}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, the peak 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{h}$
\end_inset

 of the median agent is socially optimal, i.e., it cannot be defeated by any
 other alternative, by majority voting.
 Any alternative having this property is called a Condorcet winner.
 Therefore, a Condorcet winner exists whenever the preferences of all agents
 are single-peaked with respect to the same linear order.
\end_layout

